theory.intro:
A moving particle's position vector $\underset{\sim}{r}(t)=x(t)\underset{\sim}{i}+y(t)\underset{\sim}{j}$ has an $x$ part and a $y$ part. Differentiate each part separately with respect to $t$ to get **velocity** $\underset{\sim}{v}(t)$, then again to get **acceleration** $\underset{\sim}{a}(t)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-0.6) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3:3, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.25*\x*\x});
\node[right] at (-3.3,3.0) {path of the particle};
\coordinate (P) at (2,1);
\fill (P) circle (2pt);
\node[below right] at (P) {$P$};
\draw[-{Stealth}, thick] (P) -- (3.2,2.2) node[right] {$\underset{\sim}{v}$};
\draw[-{Stealth}, thick] (P) -- (2,2.3) node[above] {$\underset{\sim}{a}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
A particle moves on the path shown, with position vector $\underset{\sim}{r}(t)=4\cos 3t\,\underset{\sim}{i}+4\sin 3t\,\underset{\sim}{j}$. Show that its speed is constant, and state that speed.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=0.6cm, y=0.6cm]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-5.6,0) -- (5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-5.6) -- (0,5.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0) circle (4);
\node[below left] at (-4,0) {$-4$};
\node[below right] at (4,0) {$4$};
\node[above left] at (0,4) {$4$};
\node[below left] at (0,-4) {$-4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
